<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rædwald of East Anglia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 624</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Raedwald of East Anglia · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuffingas">Wuffingas</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rædwald was king of East Anglia in the early 7th century and by 616 the most powerful king in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, named by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> among the overlords of the English. In 616 he defeated and killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelfrith-of-northumbria">Æthelfrith of Northumbria</a> at the River Idle, placing Edwin on the Northumbrian throne. He is widely thought to be the man commemorated by the great ship burial at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutton-hoo">Sutton Hoo</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/raedwald-of-east-anglia.jpg" alt="Rædwald of East Anglia" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mark Ramsay · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sutton_Hoo_helmet_(replica).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rædwald, whose name means 'power in counsel', was born around 560 to 580. He was the son of Tytila of East Anglia and a member of the Wuffingas dynasty, named after his grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuffa">Wuffa</a>, and the elder brother of Eni. East Anglia included the present-day counties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norfolk">Norfolk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suffolk">Suffolk</a>. Details of his reign are scarce, largely because the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> invasions of the 9th century destroyed the East Anglian monasteries where documents would have been kept. During the 590s he married a pagan woman whose name is unknown, and they had at least two sons, Rægenhere and Eorpwald. He also had an older son, Sigeberht, whom he drove into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Rædwald reigned from about 599, at first under the overlordship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelberht-of-kent">Æthelberht of Kent</a>. He became a Christian, probably around 604 or later, at Æthelberht's court, but he kept a temple with two altars, one to Christ and one to pagan gods. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a>, who disapproved, regarded him as a renouncer of the faith. For a time the only royal Christian altar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> was his, while Kent and Essex returned to paganism after the deaths of Æthelberht, who died on 24 February 616, and of Saebert of Essex.</p><p>Edwin, an exile from Northumbria, sought his protection. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelfrith-of-northumbria">Æthelfrith of Northumbria</a> sent messengers three times offering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> for Edwin's death or surrender, and Rædwald wavered, but his queen reproached him for betraying a friend and he resolved on war. He marched north with his son Rægenhere and met Æthelfrith's army on the east bank of the River Idle, on the western boundary of Lindsey. Æthelfrith and Rægenhere were both killed, and Edwin became king of Northumbria. The battle was long remembered in the saying that the river Idle was foul with the blood of Englishmen. From about 616 Rædwald was the most powerful English king south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humber">Humber</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> called him Rex Anglorum.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Rædwald died around 624. He was succeeded by his pagan son Eorpwald, who was later persuaded by Edwin to convert, and the ruling house's conversion became permanent only under Sigeberht. During his supremacy the quayside settlement at Gipeswic (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ipswich">Ipswich</a>) became an important trading centre.</p><p>Since the excavation of Mound 1 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutton-hoo">Sutton Hoo</a> in 1939, historians have considered Rædwald the most likely person buried or commemorated there, though the identification is disputed. The chamber held gold and garnet equipment and a consular sceptre, which may have symbolised the office of overlord. Other candidates include other East Anglian kings and a foreign visitor. A smaller ship-burial found in 1998 nearby is thought to contain Rægenhere.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rædwald of East Anglia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Raedwald</b>, overlord of the southern English around 616, probably lies in the Sutton Hoo ship burial, with its gold and Byzantine silver.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-east-anglia">Kingdom of East Anglia</a></li></ul></section>
